{#
   NL→IR Roadmap Items 1+5+7 — `sce:req` traceability,
   `sce:provenance` spec anchors, and `sce:unresolved` placeholder
   annotation comments.

   The parser collects `<… sce:req="ID1 ID2" …>` whitespace tokens
   and any `<sce:unresolved id="…" reason="…" candidates="…"/>` (or
   attribute-form equivalents) on `<state>`, `<transition>`,
   `<onentry>`, `<onexit>`, `<send>`, `<script>`, `<invoke>`.
   `<onentry>`/`<onexit>` block-level `sce:req` is inherited to each
   child action by `parser::inherit_req`, and `sce:provenance` the
   same way by `parser::inherit_provenance`, so emission at the
   action level captures the block annotation without a separate
   hook.

   This macro family emits the annotation as a backend-appropriate
   trailing comment so the generated source preserves the
   traceability link without any behaviour change. When `req`,
   `provenance` and `unresolved` are all empty (Vec::is_empty serde
   skip), the macros emit nothing — byte-identical output for inputs
   without annotations is preserved.

   Per-backend comment syntax:
     - cpp / rust / go / kotlin : `// sce:req: …` / `// sce:unresolved: …`
     - c (C11)                  : `/* sce:req: … */` (block form)
     - python                   : `# sce:req: …` / `# sce:unresolved: …`

   ⚠ Every author-supplied value below goes through `| comment_text`
   (`sce_build::comment_text::encode`). The annotations are opaque by
   contract (SCE_ACCEPTED_SUBSET §2.10), so an id may contain `*/` or end
   in `\`, and an unresolved reason may carry a newline. Before the
   encoder existed, each of those put author text into generated CODE:
   measured 2026-09-13, an id `A*/B` closed the C11 block comment, a
   reason with a newline broke the five line-comment backends (Python
   failed to compile; Go parsed the injected line as a statement), and a
   trailing `\` spliced the next C++ line. This header used to call the
   newline case "author-side hygiene, not a SCE invariant". It is an
   invariant: author text that is opaque by contract cannot be the
   author's job to keep out of the emitter's syntax.

   The encoding writes `\`, LF, CR, the `/` of `*/` and the `*` of `/*`
   as `\xHH` and leaves every other character alone, so an ordinary id
   is byte-identical. One grammar for all six backends, reversed by
   `comment_text::decode`. `sce_annotation_emission.rs` holds both
   halves: `hostile_annotation_text_stays_inside_its_comment` fails if
   any value reaches code, and the provenance round-trip decodes before
   it re-parses.

   Ordering is the document order surfaced by the parser
   (`RequirementId::split` preserves token order; the `unresolved`
   vector is push-order from the parse walker). The macros do not
   re-sort — call-site authors get deterministic output anchored on
   the SCXML source order.

   Spec-anchor shape — the SAME compact URI the `sce:provenance`
   attribute is written in, one line per anchor:
     `sce:provenance: doc_id[@rev][#section[:kind=value]]`

   One line per anchor rather than one joined line (the shape
   `sce:req` uses) because a `doc_id` is not whitespace-delimited:
   the element form accepts `doc-id="OEM SPEC 01"`, and a
   space-joined list of those could not be split back apart.

   The rendering is total over what the model can hold, which is
   wider than what the attribute form can spell: the element form
   can set `page` with no `section`, so the section part is emitted
   whenever EITHER is present and an empty section reads back as
   absent — `OEM-TIMING-REQ#:7` parses to exactly (doc_id, page=7).
   `SpecProvenance::parse_compact` accepts every string this emits
   once decoded, and `sce_annotation_emission.rs` re-parses each
   emitted line to hold that true rather than asserting it here.

   Unresolved-marker shape (kept compact, opaque to SCE):
     `sce:unresolved: id=<ID>[ reason=<R>][ candidates=<A,B,C>]`

   Two call patterns are supported. Pick the one that fits the
   emission site without disturbing the surrounding indentation.

   ──── Pattern A — `annotation_comment` (leading newline) ───────

       <existing line content>:{{ annot.annotation_comment(node, backend, "<indent>") }}
       <next line content>

   The macro returns either the empty string (byte-identical
   preservation when no annotations) or a sequence of complete
   lines, each prefixed with an explicit `\n` + the caller-supplied
   `<indent>` and NO trailing newline. Slotting at the end of an
   existing line keeps the post-`}}` newline as the separator to
   the next line. Good for case-label sites where the annotation
   should *attach* visually to a label or condition.

   ──── Pattern B — `annotation_block` (trailing newline) ────────

       {% for action in block %}
       {{ annot.annotation_block(action, backend, "<indent>") }}<next line content>
       {% endfor %}

   The macro returns either the empty string or a sequence of
   complete lines, each prefixed with the caller-supplied
   `<indent>` and ending with `\n`. Concatenating with the next
   expression keeps byte-identity when empty (no characters added)
   and slots the comment lines AS A BLOCK directly before the next
   content when populated. Good for emit-loop sites where the
   annotation logically *introduces* the code that follows.

   Both macros emit nothing when `node.req`, `node.provenance` and
   `node.unresolved` are all empty, and the generator runs minijinja
   with `trim_blocks=True` + `lstrip_blocks=True` so every newline
   inside the macro body is explicit (`{{ "\n" }}` or the
   inside-tag form `{% endif -%}…\n{% if …%}`) — implicit
   whitespace is unreliable under those flags.
#}

{#- One anchor in the compact URI the attribute form is written in.
    Factored out so the grammar has ONE spelling in this file: the two
    emission patterns below differ only in where their newline goes,
    and a second copy of `doc_id[@rev][#section[:page]]` is exactly
    the kind of thing that drifts on one side. -#}
{#- The position is rendered by ITERATING the variant rather than by
    naming its arms. `at` serialises as a one-key map — `{"page": 118}`,
    `{"row": 41}`, `{"path": "/Elem"}` — so `kind=value` falls out of the
    key and the value, and a fourth shape needs no edit here.

    ⚠ Written this way because the alternative already failed once: this
    macro read `p.page` by name, and when the Rust field became a
    variant the lookup silently returned nothing. Every backend went on
    emitting anchors with the coordinate quietly missing, and no
    compiler could see it — a template reads the IR by string. The
    round-trip in `sce_annotation_emission.rs` is what caught it. -#}
{%- macro provenance_compact(p) -%}
{{ p.doc_id }}{% if p.rev %}@{{ p.rev }}{% endif %}{% if p.section or p.at %}#{{ p.section | default("") }}{% if p.at %}:{% for kind, value in p.at | items %}{{ kind }}={{ value }}{% endfor %}{% endif %}{% endif %}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro annotation_comment(node, backend, indent="") -%}
{%- if node and (node.req or node.provenance or node.unresolved) -%}
{%- if backend == "c" -%}
{%- set open = "/* " -%}
{%- set close = " */" -%}
{%- elif backend == "python" -%}
{%- set open = "# " -%}
{%- set close = "" -%}
{%- else -%}
{%- set open = "// " -%}
{%- set close = "" -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if node.req -%}
{{ "\n" }}{{ indent }}{{ open }}sce:req: {{ node.req | join(" ") | comment_text }}{{ close }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- for p in node.provenance -%}
{{ "\n" }}{{ indent }}{{ open }}sce:provenance: {{ provenance_compact(p) | comment_text }}{{ close }}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- for marker in node.unresolved -%}
{{ "\n" }}{{ indent }}{{ open }}sce:unresolved: id={{ marker.id | comment_text }}{% if marker.reason %} reason={{ marker.reason | comment_text }}{% endif %}{% if marker.candidates %} candidates={{ marker.candidates | join(",") | comment_text }}{% endif %}{{ close }}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro annotation_block(node, backend, indent="") -%}
{%- if node and (node.req or node.provenance or node.unresolved) -%}
{%- if backend == "c" -%}
{%- set open = "/* " -%}
{%- set close = " */" -%}
{%- elif backend == "python" -%}
{%- set open = "# " -%}
{%- set close = "" -%}
{%- else -%}
{%- set open = "// " -%}
{%- set close = "" -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if node.req -%}
{{ indent }}{{ open }}sce:req: {{ node.req | join(" ") | comment_text }}{{ close }}{{ "\n" }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- for p in node.provenance -%}
{{ indent }}{{ open }}sce:provenance: {{ provenance_compact(p) | comment_text }}{{ close }}{{ "\n" }}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- for marker in node.unresolved -%}
{{ indent }}{{ open }}sce:unresolved: id={{ marker.id | comment_text }}{% if marker.reason %} reason={{ marker.reason | comment_text }}{% endif %}{% if marker.candidates %} candidates={{ marker.candidates | join(",") | comment_text }}{% endif %}{{ close }}{{ "\n" }}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
